/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ammonia03_quench_converter -- the SAME synthesis loop as ammonia02, with the
  converter redesigned as THREE ADIABATIC BEDS WITH COLD-SHOT QUENCH.
  ===================================================================
  makeup 25 bar ─[T101]─[makeupComp 25->245]─┐
                                             ▼
                        recycle ─[recycleComp]─►[mixer]
                                                │ mixed  (26645 kmol/h, 386 K)
                                                ▼
                                        [quenchSplit]  <-- THE DESIGN VARIABLE
                              47 %│          25 %│          28 %│
                             toFEHE│       quench1│       quench2│
                                   ▼              │              │
                             [FEHE] ◄══ hotEffluent (heat recovery)
                                   │ preheatedFeed 683 K          │
                                   ▼              │              │
                              [bed1] ──841 K──►[quench1Mix]◄─────┘ (682 K)
                                                 │ bed2Feed
                                                 ▼
                              [bed2] ──798 K──►[quench2Mix]◄──────── (681 K)
                                                 │ bed3Feed
                                                 ▼
                              [bed3] ──778 K──► hotEffluent ──► [FEHE] hot side
                                                                    │
                                                     [waterCooler] ─┘
                                                            ▼
                                                     [separator] 250 K / 200 bar
                                                       │        │
                                                   rawLiquid  unreactedGas
                                                       ▼            ▼
                                                 [letdown]     [purgeSplit]
                                                  │     │       │        │
                                           productNH3  gas    purge   recycle (tear)

  WHY QUENCH.  N2 + 3 H2 <-> 2 NH3 is EXOTHERMIC and REVERSIBLE.  Exothermic
  means an adiabatic bed heats itself as it converts; reversible means the
  equilibrium it is climbing towards RETREATS as it heats.  A single adiabatic
  bed therefore runs up one straight line in the (conversion, temperature)
  plane until it meets the equilibrium curve, and stops there -- ammonia02
  stops at 839.6 K and 10.75 % NH3.  A quench converter cuts the bed into
  pieces and drops the gas back down the temperature axis between them, with
  cold loop gas taken off upstream of the interchanger.  The gas ZIGZAGS: up
  the adiabatic line inside a bed, vertically down at each cold shot.

  MEASURED, this run against ammonia02 (identical feed, pressure, purge and
  separator; only the converter and the interchanger differ):

      converter exit        10.75 % NH3 @ 839.6 K   ->   17.28 % NH3 @ 777.5 K
      per-pass N2 conv.           17.84 %           ->        28.80 %
      loop circulation        40545 kmol/h          ->    26645 kmol/h
      recycle                 32545 kmol/h          ->    18645 kmol/h
      purge (3 % of recycle)   1006 kmol/h          ->      577 kmol/h
      product                  3498.7 kmol/h        ->     3719.8 kmol/h  (+6.3 %)

  The product gain is NOT the extra equilibrium conversion by itself: it is
  that a loop which converts more per pass recirculates less, so the 3 % purge
  throws away 43 % less hydrogen.

  THE SPLIT IS THE DESIGN.  47 / 25 / 28 is not a given; it is chosen.  With
  the interchanger fixed at 375 m2, lowering the FEHE branch sends a smaller
  stream through the same area and preheats bed 1 MORE while cooling beds 2
  and 3; raising it does the opposite.  47 % is the value at which all three
  beds enter within 2.6 K of each other, at the 683 K that ammonia02's own
  converter already uses as its light-off inlet.  Read README.md for the
  measurements, including what a BAD split costs and why a fourth bed is not
  obviously worth building.

  WHAT THIS MODEL DOES NOT CONTAIN -- read this before quoting any number.
  Every bed here is a `gibbsReactor`: it runs to FULL EQUILIBRIUM, which is
  the same as saying it is infinitely long.  There is no rate law, no catalyst
  volume, no particle size and no approach to equilibrium anywhere in this
  case, so:
    * no bed can be SIZED, and system/postDict declares all three so that the
      engine refuses them by name on every run;
    * nothing here can tell a good injection POINT from a bad one.  Measured:
      moving quench from 25/28 to 50/3 leaves the converter exit unchanged to
      five figures, because with the last bed at equilibrium the outlet
      depends only on the TOTAL cold shot, not on where it enters.  That is
      an exact property of the model, not a numerical accident -- and it is
      exactly the question a real design has to answer.

  THE CONSTRUCTION IS DYSON AND SIMON'S.  The rate contours in the
  conversion-temperature plane, the locus of maximum rate descending as
  conversion rises, and a cold-shot trajectory drawn as a zigzag about it, are
  Figure 2 of D. C. Dyson and J. M. Simon, "A kinetic expression with
  diffusion correction for ammonia synthesis on industrial catalyst",
  Ind. Eng. Chem. Fundam. 7 (1968) 605-610.  This case executes that picture
  with equilibrium beds; their rate law is what would put the locus on it.

  UNCHANGED FROM ammonia02, deliberately: 200 bar synthesis, makeup 1980
  kmol/h N2 + 5940 kmol/h H2 (exactly 3:1) + 80 kmol/h Ar, both compressors
  and their shaft powers, the water cooler, the 250 K / 200 bar refrigerated
  separator, the 20 bar let-down, the 3 % purge, the two storage tanks, the
  declared cooling-water circuit, and the SRK + Henry thermophysical package
  with its cited N2-NH3 and H2-NH3 pairs.

  CHANGED, and each change is a consequence of the converter:
    * the INTERCHANGER falls from 1500 m2 to 375 m2.  It carries 47 % of the
      loop gas instead of 100 %, from an effluent 62 K cooler.  Its duty falls
      from 117.2 MW to 31.2 MW.
    * the WATER COOLER takes 66.2 MW instead of 57.8 MW: 8.4 MW that the
      interchanger no longer recovers leaves through the cooling water
      instead.  A real plant puts a waste-heat boiler on that effluent.  This
      case does not add one -- that is a separate design act, and inventing it
      here would change two things at once.
    * the recycle TOLERANCE is 1e-8 instead of 1e-5.  At 1e-5 this loop's
      element balance closed only to 0.05 %; three reactors on one tear leave
      more room for a loose tear to show.
  NOT changed and worth seeing: the recycle compressor still takes 4000 kW on
  a circulation 43 % smaller, so it now discharges at 258 bar into a mixer
  that runs at 245.  The circulator is oversized for the converter it feeds --
  a real consequence of the redesign, left visible rather than tuned away.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only
units
(
    { name T101;  type storageTank;  in makeup;  outputs ( makeupStored );
      operation { residenceTime 0.5 h; } }

    { name makeupComp;  type compressor;  in makeupStored;   outputs ( compMakeup );
      operation { W_shaft 24000 kW;  eta 0.78; } }

    { name recycleComp; type compressor;  in recycle;  outputs ( compRecycle );
      operation { W_shaft 4000 kW;   eta 0.80; } }

    { name mixer;  type mixer;  inputs ( compMakeup  compRecycle );  outputs ( mixed ); }

    //  THE DESIGN VARIABLE.  The cold shots are drawn here, UPSTREAM of the
    //  interchanger, so they reach the beds at loop-inlet temperature (386 K)
    //  rather than at the 683 K the interchanger delivers.  Change these three
    //  numbers and you have redesigned the converter; nothing else in the case
    //  has to move.
    { name quenchSplit;  type splitter;  in mixed;
      outputs ( toFEHE  quench1  quench2 );
      operation { fractions ( 0.47  0.25  0.28 ); } }

    { name FEHE;  type heatExchanger;  inputs ( hotEffluent  toFEHE );
      outputs ( cooledEffluent  preheatedFeed );
      operation { area 375 m2;  U 550 W/m2/K;  flow counter; } }

    //  BED 1 -- the coldest feed and the leanest gas, so the steepest climb:
    //  683 -> 841 K, and it alone accounts for 1075 of the 3674 kmol/h of
    //  ammonia made per pass.  `T` is the outer Newton's GUESS, not a spec:
    //  in adiabatic mode the temperature is what the run computes.
    { name bed1;  type gibbsReactor;  in preheatedFeed;  outputs ( bed1Out );
      operation
      {
          mode adiabatic;  P 200 bar;  T 840 K;
          elements ( N  H  Ar );
          species ( { name N2; atoms ( 2 0 0 ); } { name H2; atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
                    { name NH3; atoms ( 1 3 0 ); } { name Ar; atoms ( 0 0 1 ); } );
      } }

    //  COLD SHOT 1.  25 % of the loop gas at 386 K meets the bed-1 effluent at
    //  841 K and takes it back to 682 K -- the vertical leg of the zigzag.  It
    //  also DILUTES: the ammonia fraction drops from 10.61 % to 7.12 %, which
    //  moves the gas away from equilibrium as well as down in temperature.
    { name quench1Mix;  type mixer;  inputs ( bed1Out  quench1 );  outputs ( bed2Feed ); }

    { name bed2;  type gibbsReactor;  in bed2Feed;  outputs ( bed2Out );
      operation
      {
          mode adiabatic;  P 200 bar;  T 800 K;
          elements ( N  H  Ar );
          species ( { name N2; atoms ( 2 0 0 ); } { name H2; atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
                    { name NH3; atoms ( 1 3 0 ); } { name Ar; atoms ( 0 0 1 ); } );
      } }

    //  COLD SHOT 2 is LARGER than cold shot 1 (28 % against 25 %) even though
    //  bed 2's temperature rise is SMALLER (116 K against 158 K): bed 2 is
    //  carrying half again as much gas, so the heat to be removed is larger.
    { name quench2Mix;  type mixer;  inputs ( bed2Out  quench2 );  outputs ( bed3Feed ); }

    //  BED 3 -- the whole loop gas, the richest feed and the lowest exit
    //  temperature, and it still makes the most ammonia of the three
    //  (1391 kmol/h) because it works at the coolest equilibrium.
    { name bed3;  type gibbsReactor;  in bed3Feed;  outputs ( hotEffluent );
      operation
      {
          mode adiabatic;  P 200 bar;  T 780 K;
          elements ( N  H  Ar );
          species ( { name N2; atoms ( 2 0 0 ); } { name H2; atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
                    { name NH3; atoms ( 1 3 0 ); } { name Ar; atoms ( 0 0 1 ); } );
      } }

    { name waterCooler;  type heatExchanger;  inputs ( cooledEffluent  cw );
      outputs ( chilledEffluent  cwOut );
      operation { area 4000 m2;  U 700 W/m2/K;  flow counter; } }

    { name separator;  type isothermalFlash;  in chilledEffluent;
      outputs ( rawLiquid  unreactedGas );  operation { T 250 K;  P 200 bar; } }

    { name letdown;  type isothermalFlash;  in rawLiquid;
      outputs ( productNH3  letdownGas );  operation { T 250 K;  P 20 bar; } }

    { name purgeSplit;  type splitter;  in unreactedGas;  outputs ( purge  recycle );
      operation { fractions ( 0.03  0.97 ); } }

    { name T201;  type storageTank;  in productNH3;  outputs ( productStored );
      operation { residenceTime 48 h; } }
);

//  THE COOLING WATER IS A DECLARED CIRCUIT, NOT PROCESS MATERIAL -- exactly as
//  in ammonia02, and for the same reason: at 9007.5 t/h it is 99.2 % of this
//  plant's boundary mass, so a closure computed over it is passing by
//  dilution.  The declaration buys a second, PROCESS scope in the plant
//  summary; it removes the circuit from no physical check.
utilities
(
    {
        name     CW1;
        service  coolingWater;
        supply   cw;
        return   cwOut;
        note     "closed cooling-water circuit: tower supply at 25 C, return at 35 C";
    }
);
